    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d22">

                <pb facs="0055" n="39" sameAs="frus:frus1948v09/pg39"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">821.00/4–948: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Colombia</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Beaulac</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Acting Secretary of
                            State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Bogotá</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1948-04-09T15:00:00-05:00">April 9, 1948—3 p.
                            m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>190. Liberal Party chief Jorge Gaitan was shot and killed about 1:15 p. m. today
                    on Carrera Septima and Jimenez de Quesada in central Bogotá. Mob seized and
                    killed assassin, dragged corpse to front Presidential Palace, then hanged it on
                    public street. Mob invaded Capitolio, seat of Pan American Conference,
                    ransacking building and attempting set fire at least one wing. Within one hour
                    after Gaitan’s assassination, armed individuals and bands began looting shops,
                    with determined attacks on hardware shops to obtain weapons, including machetes,
                    iron pipes, guns, etc. Bomb was thrown into ground floor Edificio Americano
                    where offices U.S. delegation housed on seventh floor.<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d22-f1"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Beaulac</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d22-f1" n="1" xml:id="d22-f1-content">
                <p>For an article entitled “Bogotá, April 9”, by Cecil B. Lyon, Special Assistant to
                    the Assistant Secretary for Political Affairs (Armour), and Adviser to the
                    United States Delegation, see <hi rendition="#italic">The American Foreign
                        Service Journal</hi>, vol. 25, No. 5, May 1948, p. 7. See also USDel Report, <ref target="#pg6">pp. 6</ref>–<ref
                        target="#pg8">8</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
